{"reference":"Luke 10","book":"Luke","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Mercy and the Neighbor","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jesus answers the lawyer’s attempt to limit the meaning of “neighbor” by making mercy concrete: the Samaritan acts for the wounded man when others do not. The story presents compassion as crossing a boundary rather than as an abstract definition, and it should not be used to make real Samaritans a mere foil for supposedly deficient Judaism.","verse_refs":["Luke 10:25-37"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_010_d532da3a5aea6f2d740be12a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions Raised by the Chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jesus’ statement that he saw Satan fall from heaven should not automatically be read as a direct reference to Isaiah’s “morning star, son of dawn.” In Isaiah, that poem is framed as a taunt against the king of Babylon; identifying its figure with Satan belongs to later Jewish and especially Christian reception rather than to Isaiah’s explicit addressee.","verse_refs":["Luke 10:18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_010_022ae806a857052c99f975d3"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"“The laborer is worthy of his hire” recognizes that those sent out to work may receive provision. A related, disputed discussion of “double honor” for elders understands honor as possibly including remuneration, while also cautioning that such support does not create clerical entitlement or place leaders beyond scrutiny.","verse_refs":["Luke 10:7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_010_326113cc60b4b940b9bfc631"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Mary’s listening and Martha’s burdened hospitality create a scene about attention, anxiety, work, and discipleship. Jesus’ response need not demean domestic labor or confine women to a single role; the passage contrasts Martha’s troubled distraction with Mary’s chosen attention to Jesus’ teaching.","verse_refs":["Luke 10:38-42"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_010_78e374d859a4c8acc9f25456"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}